  "query_preview": "/Sunday through Wednesday/... \nSome variation of a Rex or omega block will define the southern \nstream flow pattern early next week as broad ridging dominates the\nnorthern Conus and northern stream jet energy retreats into \nsouthern Canada. While there are certainly differences among \nensemble members regarding the exact placement and intensity of \nquasi-cutoff features in the southern stream, this synoptic \npattern will promote surface pressure rises over the intermountain\nWest and dry N/NE boundary layer flow. As a result, periods of \nbreezy east winds are likely at times over the eastern CWA (very \ncommon occurrence this time of year), though speeds should not be \nparticularly strong or unusual. At some point early next week, \nmost ensemble members suggest the aforementioned cutoff feature \nopening and residual energy moving through the region, though it \nremains to be seen whether an introduction of mid/upper level \nmoisture can overcome the dry boundary layer airmass. The greatest\nodds and large preponderance of ensemble membership indicate dry \nweather persisting through the middle of the week. Forecast \nconfidence is very good that H5 heights will remain nearly \nconstant in a 580-584dm range resulting in a near persistence \ntemperature forecast with readings very near climatology.",
